I've known subconsciously for a while now that slow monsters' movement
cycles allow you to attack them a few times, and then move away on the
turn on which they'd normally attack you, so that they instead just
move to the space right next to you where you can repeat this process.
I learned it by necessity from several unfortunate encounters with
groups of manes. (Did manes used to be stronger in an earlier
version?)
  Anyway, in my last game I started exploiting this as much as I
could. Learn how many turns there are between a wood golem's attacks,
and he'll never even get a chance to take a swing... Even though it's
not really an opportunity that presents itself very often, it seems
like this is a bit too exploitable. It's exploiting the algorithms of
the game, even, not just some piece of knowledge learned from playing
and getting wiser. What I mean to say is that it's not something you
(as a character) learn like "don't attack floating eyes without
reflection or blindness," but rather a pattern you (as a player)
notice in the mechanics of the game, which is worse.

  What I'm curious about is: Is there a name for this "dancing" trick?
Is it considered an undesirable behavior of the game? (Dare I use the
word "bug?") Perhaps slow monsters' movement cycles could be slightly
randomized so they're not totally predictable?

